<Doc title="Resistor colour code" subtitle="the bands, and a worked example" category="Parts" id="resistorColours">

<Draw width="640" height="250">
setColor white
setTextSize 10
drawText 20 7 "colour"
drawText 150 7 "digit"
drawText 230 7 "as multiplier"

# The swatches are literal #RRGGBB. These are the resistor colour CODE, a
# real-world standard: the palette governs the interface, not the physics it
# depicts. Each swatch is outlined in the page rule so a dark band still reads
# as a band.

# ---- black
setColor #181818
fillRect 20 19 132 37
setColor grid
setWidth 1
drawRect 20 19 132 37
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 11
drawText 26 22 "black"
drawText 150 22 "0"
setColor white
drawText 230 22 "x1"

# ---- brown
setColor #6B4222
fillRect 20 41 132 59
setColor grid
drawRect 20 41 132 59
setColor brWhite
drawText 26 44 "brown"
drawText 150 44 "1"
setColor white
drawText 230 44 "x10"

# ---- red
setColor #B03026
fillRect 20 63 132 81
setColor grid
drawRect 20 63 132 81
setColor brWhite
drawText 26 66 "red"
drawText 150 66 "2"
setColor white
drawText 230 66 "x100"

# ---- orange
setColor #D07020
fillRect 20 85 132 103
setColor grid
drawRect 20 85 132 103
setColor black
drawText 26 88 "orange"
setColor brWhite
drawText 150 88 "3"
setColor white
drawText 230 88 "x1k"

# ---- yellow
setColor #D8C030
fillRect 20 107 132 125
setColor grid
drawRect 20 107 132 125
setColor black
drawText 26 110 "yellow"
setColor brWhite
drawText 150 110 "4"
setColor white
drawText 230 110 "x10k"

# ---- green
setColor #3C8A40
fillRect 20 129 132 147
setColor grid
drawRect 20 129 132 147
setColor brWhite
drawText 26 132 "green"
drawText 150 132 "5"
setColor white
drawText 230 132 "x100k"

# ---- blue
setColor #335CA8
fillRect 20 151 132 169
setColor grid
drawRect 20 151 132 169
setColor brWhite
drawText 26 154 "blue"
drawText 150 154 "6"
setColor white
drawText 230 154 "x1M"

# ---- violet
setColor #7A46A0
fillRect 20 173 132 191
setColor grid
drawRect 20 173 132 191
setColor brWhite
drawText 26 176 "violet"
drawText 150 176 "7"
setColor white
drawText 230 176 "x10M"

# ---- grey
setColor #868686
fillRect 20 195 132 213
setColor grid
drawRect 20 195 132 213
setColor brWhite
drawText 26 198 "grey"
drawText 150 198 "8"
setColor white
drawText 230 198 "-"

# ---- white
setColor #DCDCDC
fillRect 20 217 132 235
setColor grid
drawRect 20 217 132 235
setColor black
drawText 26 220 "white"
setColor brWhite
drawText 150 220 "9"
setColor white
drawText 230 220 "-"

# ---- a worked example, since the table alone never quite lands.
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 12
drawText 360 6 "reading one"

setColor yellow
setWidth 1
drawRect 360 40 560 88

# The colour code again - see the swatches above for why these stay as they are.
setColor #B03026
fillRect 384 40 398 88
setColor #B03026
fillRect 414 40 428 88
setColor #6B4222
fillRect 444 40 458 88

# The gold band, set apart at the far end of the body.
setColor #C0A050
fillRect 528 40 540 88

setColor white
setTextSize 11
drawText 360 99 "red  red  brown"
setColor brWhite
drawText 360 117 "2    2    x10   = 220 ohm"
setColor grey
setTextSize 10
drawText 360 141 "The gold band on its own is tolerance,"
drawText 360 155 "and it marks the END you read TOWARD."
</Draw>

<Note><Bold>The gold band on its own is tolerance, and it marks the END you read
TOWARD.</Bold> It is what tells you which of the two ends is the first band.</Note>

<Note><Bold>For an LED on 3.3 V, anything from 220 ohm to 1k is fine.</Bold>
Higher means dimmer, lower means brighter and hotter.</Note>

</Doc>
